<%# What They Do tab of the Life in the Wild page %>
<p class="page-subtitle">Elephants are smart. They live in close families, talk to each other and look after one another. The way an animal acts is called its behavior.</p>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How elephants get food</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li data-mascot-fact="An elephant can pick up a single tortilla chip with its trunk without breaking it.">
      <span class="fact-label">Trunk</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Pulls up grass, picks fruit and leaves, and reaches branches 6 m high. That's about as high as a giraffe!</span>
      <%= copy_button "Trunk" %>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="About a third of each tusk is hidden inside the elephant&#x27;s skull.">
      <span class="fact-label">Tusks</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Peel bark off trees and dig up roots.</span>
      <%= copy_button "Tusks" %>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Feet</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Kick up clumps of grass and dig in dry riverbeds to find water.</span>
      <%= copy_button "Feet" %>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="An elephant&#x27;s trunk can lift objects weighing around 350 kg.">
      <span class="fact-label">Strength</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Push over whole trees to reach the leaves and fruit at the top.</span>
      <%= copy_button "Strength" %>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="During a severe drought in Tanzania in 1993, families led by older matriarchs were more likely to survive, probably because they remembered where to find water.">
      <span class="fact-label">Memory</span>
      <span class="fact-value">The grandma elephant who leads the family remembers where to find food and water in each season, even many years later.</span>
      <%= copy_button "Memory" %>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How elephants stay safe</h2>
  <p>
    Grown-up elephants are so big that very few animals try to hunt them. But lions, hyenas and crocodiles in Africa,
    and tigers in Asia, sometimes go after baby elephants. The biggest danger to elephants is people. Some people hunt
    elephants for their tusks, even though it's against the law, and others take over the land where elephants live.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants have been seen rushing to help a calf calling in distress, even when it isn&#x27;t their own.">
      <h3>Strength in numbers</h3>
      <p>When threatened, the herd forms a defensive circle with the calves safely in the middle.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Strength in numbers" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="A real charge is usually silent, with ears pinned back and trunk tucked in, very different from a noisy mock charge.">
      <h3>Warning displays</h3>
      <p>Spread ears, a raised trunk, loud trumpeting and mock charges are usually enough to scare predators away.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Warning displays" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="In Botswana&#x27;s Chobe region, some lion prides have learned to hunt young elephants at night.">
      <h3>Tusks and size</h3>
      <p>If a threat doesn't back down, an elephant can charge at around 25 km/h and fight back with its tusks and its sheer weight.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Tusks and size" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants flee from recordings of buzzing bees and make a special rumble that warns others about them.">
      <h3>Alarm calls</h3>
      <p>Elephants make different alarm calls for different dangers. Some research suggests they have a specific call for humans.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Alarm calls" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Sleeping</h2>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants sometimes nap leaning against a tree or a termite mound.">
      <div class="stat-label">In the wild</div>
      <div class="stat-value">About 2 hours</div>
      <p>Of sleep a day, mostly early in the morning.</p>
      <%= copy_button "In the wild" %>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="Lying down lets elephants reach REM sleep, the stage when dreaming happens.">
      <div class="stat-label">In zoos</div>
      <div class="stat-value">4–6 hours</div>
      <p>Of sleep a night, because there are no wild animals to watch out for.</p>
      <%= copy_button "In zoos" %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="info-block">
    <p>
      Wild elephants sleep less than almost any other mammal. They usually sleep standing up! They only lie down for a
      deeper sleep every few days.
    </p>
    <%= copy_button "Sleeping" %>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Record breakers:</strong> Scientists in Botswana found that some elephant leaders stayed awake for up to
      46 hours in a row. That's almost two whole days!
    </p>
    <%= copy_button "Record breakers" %>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>More amazing things elephants do</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="When family members meet after time apart, they greet each other with a noisy celebration of rumbles, trumpets and flapping ears.">
      <h3>Family life</h3>
      <p>An elephant family is made up of mothers, aunts, sisters and babies, led by the grandma. When boys become teenagers, they leave to live alone or with other males.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Family life" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="A 2024 study found that elephants seem to call each other by name, using unique rumbles for specific individuals.">
      <h3>Communication</h3>
      <p>Low rumbles, many too deep for humans to hear, can travel several kilometres through the air and the ground.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Communication" %>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants help fallen family members back to their feet, nudging and lifting them with their trunks and tusks.">
      <h3>Mourning</h3>
      <p>Elephants have been seen gently touching the bones of dead elephants and standing quietly near them, as if grieving.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Mourning" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
